    "content": "<p>Wavefront Studio builds websites, SEO and lead systems for Miami businesses. We are a small studio in Sarasota, Florida, working with you remotely.</p>\n<div class=\"lf-cta\"><p>Not sure how you rank in Miami right now?</p><p>We will audit your site for free and tell you what is actually holding it back in Miami — including if the answer is that you do not need a rebuild.</p><div class=\"lf-buttons\"><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"/contact/\">Get a free audit</a></div><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"tel:+19414152595\">Call (941) 415-2595</a></div></div></div>\n<h2>Same state, same clock, real distance</h2>\n<p>Sarasota to Miami runs about 230 highway miles, roughly three and a half hours by car each way, so a same-day site visit is not something that happens. We have no office there. All communication, project management and delivery happen digitally.</p>\n<p>What that distance does not cost you is time zone friction. Miami and Sarasota both sit on Eastern time, so a 9am call is 9am for both of us, with no early start or late finish on either end the way there would be working with a business out west. There is also, unlike Florida&#8217;s own Panhandle, nothing to double check before you book a call.</p>\n<h2>Your site is bilingual whether you planned it or not</h2>\n<p>43.9% of people aged five and over in the Miami metro area speak Spanish at home, against 13.9% nationally (American Community Survey 2024 one-year estimates), more than three times the national rate and higher than any other Florida market in our data. That is not a niche audience to accommodate later. It is close to half your addressable market.</p>\n<p>A Miami site that treats a Spanish page as an afterthought, run through a translator and bolted onto the English one, is leaving a large share of local searches and a large share of trust on the table. The businesses that do this well write the Spanish version as its own page, in Spanish, rather than converting the English copy sentence by sentence.</p>\n<h2>The other numbers worth knowing</h2>\n<p>Self-employment in the Miami metro area runs at 15.5%, against 9.9% nationally, the highest rate anywhere in our data, including Florida&#8217;s own statewide figure of 12.7%. That points to the same crowded-competition dynamic as the rest of the state, only sharper: more of the businesses you are up against online are one or two people working for themselves than you would find in most markets. Median age, broadband access and household income in Miami sit close enough to the national numbers that they should not change how a site gets built. The gap that matters here is language and competition, not any of the rest.</p>\n<h2>Brickell is not Wynwood</h2>\n<p>Miami-Dade is not one search market. Brickell functions as the region&#8217;s financial district, dense with law firms, wealth managers and corporate services selling to other businesses. Wynwood built its identity on street art and galleries and now carries a cluster of creative agencies, breweries and independent retail around that reputation. Little Havana runs on Cuban heritage and small independent food and retail. Coral Gables is its own city, built around Miracle Mile retail and a concentration of professional offices. Doral, next to Miami International Airport, is built around logistics, import-export and airport-adjacent commerce.</p>\n<p>A law firm in Brickell and a restaurant in Little Havana should not be optimising for the same handful of &#8220;Miami&#8221; keywords, because the people searching for each are not looking for the same thing.</p>\n<h2>A gateway, not just a city</h2>\n<p>Two figures explain a lot about the customers Miami businesses actually see. Miami International Airport carried 55.3 million passengers in 2025 and offers more flights to Latin America and the Caribbean than any other US airport. PortMiami logged a record 8.56 million cruise passengers in its 2025 fiscal year alongside 1.1 million cargo containers, an eleventh straight year above the million mark.</p>\n<p>Together they mean a meaningful share of the people and businesses moving through Miami arrive from, or trade with, Latin America. It is part of why the bilingual point above matters as much as it does, and why it can pay to treat visiting Latin American buyers as a specific audience rather than folding them into a generic tourist category. A business selling to residents, one selling to visitors and one selling into trade each need a different version of the same website.</p>\n<h2>What we build</h2>\n<p><a href=\"/web-development/\">Websites</a> built bilingual from the first draft, not translated after the fact. <a href=\"/seo-service/\">SEO</a> structured around Miami&#8217;s actual neighbourhoods rather than one broad target. <a href=\"/digital-marketing/\">Digital marketing</a> and <a href=\"/social-media-strategy/\">social media</a> for a market where a lot of buying decisions still move through referral and reputation as much as search. <a href=\"/lead-capture/\">Lead capture</a> and follow-up that does not let a Friday afternoon enquiry go cold over the weekend. And <a href=\"/graphic-design/\">graphic design</a> for businesses whose branding needs to work in two languages, not one.</p>\n<h2>Common questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Do you have anyone local in Miami?</strong> No. We are a small studio in Sarasota and would work with you remotely, on the same time zone, which removes most of the friction that usually comes with a remote agency.</p>\n<p><strong>Will you actually write the Spanish version, or just run it through a translator?</strong> We build it as its own page, translated by a human translator rather than a machine, so it reads the way a native speaker would write it.</p>\n<p><strong>My business is bilingual but my current site is English only. Where do we start?</strong> Usually with whichever page earns you the most enquiries today, rebuilt as two real pages rather than one page with a language switcher bolted on.</p>\n<p><strong>Do you work with businesses that sell into Latin America, not only to local customers?</strong> Yes. In a gateway market like Miami, that is worth planning for from the start.</p>\n<h2>Talk to us</h2>\n<p>Call <strong>(941) 415-2595</strong>, email <strong>info@wavefrontstudiollc.com</strong>, or <a href=\"/contact/\">book a free consultation</a>. We are at 4363 Independence Ct, Sarasota, about three and a half hours up the coast from Miami, on the same Eastern time as you.</p>\n<div class=\"lf-cta\"><div class=\"lf-buttons\"><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"/contact/\">Get a free audit</a></div><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"tel:+19414152595\">Call (941) 415-2595</a></div></div></div>\n<p>Nearby: <a href=\"/web-design-florida/\">Florida</a> · <a href=\"/web-design-sarasota-fl/\">Sarasota</a> · <a href=\"/locations/\">all locations</a></p>\n<hr/>"
