    "content": "<p>Wavefront Studio builds websites, SEO and lead systems for small businesses in the New York City area. We work with you remotely from Sarasota, Florida.</p><div class=\"lf-cta\"><p>Not sure how you rank in New York City right now?</p><p>We will audit your site for free and tell you what is actually holding it back in New York City — including if the answer is that you do not need a rebuild.</p><div class=\"lf-buttons\"><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"/contact/\">Get a free audit</a></div><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"tel:+19414152595\">Call (941) 415-2595</a></div></div></div><h2>No office in any borough, and nothing to manage across time zones</h2><p>New York City and Sarasota both run on Eastern time, so there is no scheduling gap to plan around, only distance. What we do not have is a desk anywhere in the five boroughs, and this page will not pretend otherwise with a borrowed mailing address or a coworking day pass. Communication, project management and delivery all happen digitally, the same as for every market we work in remotely.</p><p>What that arrangement genuinely cannot do: be at your Brooklyn storefront on a Tuesday morning, or walk your Long Island City studio with a camera before a launch. If either of those matters more to you than the work itself, a firm with people on the ground here is the right call.</p><h2>Twenty million people, and a keyword you cannot win by being generic</h2><p>The Census Bureau&#8217;s New York metro area, officially New York-Newark-Jersey City, holds close to 19.94 million people and reaches into two other states: Newark and Jersey City in New Jersey, and, at its rural edge, Pike County in Pennsylvania. For a small business the size itself is not the useful number. What matters is what that size does to search. &#8220;New York&#8221; as a keyword is contested by national brands, directories and review aggregators with budgets no small business can match.</p><p>The small businesses that do show up in local search usually rank for their block, their subway stop, or their specific service in their specific neighbourhood. A dentist in Park Slope competes for &#8220;Park Slope dentist&#8221;, not for &#8220;New York dentist&#8221;, and the second phrase is not a stepping stone to the first.</p><h2>What the numbers say once you look past the size</h2><p>At $99,852, the metro area&#8217;s median household income runs roughly 22% above the $81,604 national figure (American Community Survey 2024 one-year estimates). That is a market that can support higher-value positioning; a site that leads with &#8220;affordable&#8221; is competing on the one dimension where it has the least room to win here.</p><p>About 20.5% of people aged five and over in the metro area speak Spanish at home, against 13.9% nationally, a real gap worth building for directly rather than bolting on later. The share of households whose only computing device is a smartphone, 8.8%, sits below the 9.9% national rate, which tracks with a market that does a lot of research on a laptop or desktop before it ever picks up a phone: a fast, thorough desktop experience is not optional here.</p><h2>Five boroughs, and they do not behave like one city</h2><p>Brooklyn is the largest borough at just over 2.7 million people, followed by Queens at about 2.4 million, Manhattan at roughly 1.7 million, the Bronx at about 1.5 million, and Staten Island at just under half a million (2020 census, with the city as a whole estimated at 8.48 million by 2024). Each of those has its own commercial character, and a service business in one rarely competes with the same business three boroughs over.</p><p>Manhattan below 60th Street has also changed in a way that is worth knowing if you operate there: congestion pricing has cut daily vehicle entries by roughly 82,000, about 13% fewer than the MTA estimates would otherwise enter the zone. That is a real shift in how customers physically reach a storefront in that zone, and it has nothing to do with how they find you online, which is exactly why the two should not be confused in your marketing plan. If your business sits outside the five boroughs entirely, in the wider New York State market, our <a href=\"/web-design-new-york/\">New York State guide</a> covers that ground instead.</p><h2>What we build</h2><p><a href=\"/web-development/\">Web development</a> built one neighbourhood or borough at a time. A homepage that tries to speak for Staten Island and the Bronx equally usually ends up speaking to neither.</p><p><a href=\"/seo-service/\">SEO</a> aimed at the specific searches your actual customers use, since city-wide terms are the ones you are least likely to win here.</p><p><a href=\"/graphic-design/\">Graphic design</a> and brand work that reads as native to a specific part of the city rather than generic and interchangeable with a suburb three states away.</p><p><a href=\"/mobile-app-development/\">Mobile apps</a> and <a href=\"/ai-chatbot/\">AI chatbots</a> built for a customer base that researches heavily before contacting anyone, and expects a fast, complete answer the first time.</p><h2>Common questions</h2><p><strong>Do you have an office in Manhattan or any other borough?</strong> No, and we are not going to rent a mailbox to imply one. We are in Sarasota, Florida, and would work with you remotely, the same as with every market outside our driving range.</p><p><strong>Should I target all five boroughs or just my neighbourhood?</strong> Your neighbourhood, almost always. Five-borough targeting makes sense for a handful of citywide service businesses; for everyone else it dilutes the pages that would otherwise rank.</p><p><strong>Does congestion pricing change how I should market my business?</strong> It can change how customers reach you physically if you are below 60th Street, but it has no bearing on search visibility. Treat them as separate problems.</p><p><strong>My business is technically in New Jersey but serves Manhattan clients. Does that matter?</strong> For search, yes: your address and service-area settings need to reflect where you actually are, even while your content speaks to where your customers are.</p><p><strong>What does it cost?</strong> Websites start in the low five figures. SEO is monthly. We quote after a conversation.</p><h2>Talk to us</h2><p>Call <strong>(941) 415-2595</strong>, email <strong>info@wavefrontstudiollc.com</strong>, or <a href=\"/contact/\">book a free consultation</a>. We are at 4363 Independence Ct, Sarasota, Florida.</p><div class=\"lf-cta\"><div class=\"lf-buttons\"><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"/contact/\">Get a free audit</a></div><div class=\"lf-button\"><a class=\"lf-button-link\" href=\"tel:+19414152595\">Call (941) 415-2595</a></div></div></div><p>Nearby: <a href=\"/web-design-new-york/\">New York State</a> · <a href=\"/locations/\">all locations</a></p><hr/>"
